11. Lemma BP (the parity refinement) and the (9,4) counterexample
-----------------------------------------------------------------
  PASS  q <= 3r is equivalent to n <= 2r+1, so for n >= 2r+2 fewer than q colours can be parity-constrained (Lemma BP)
  PASS  9418500 (X,W) pairs on 6 random n=13 instances: every one has a colour of unconstrained parity, so the parity refinement never applies
      Pi(9,4): |X||W| = 9 is odd while every colour forces an even contribution (total parity 0)
      exhaustive UNSAT in 185 nodes; CDCL agrees; and the fractional relaxation is feasible with an exact rational point
  PASS  a class-B-type instance with NO completion whose obstruction is pure parity and is invisible to every linear relaxation
      s= 3: Pi(9,4), q=12, mu = n-r = 5, parity violated
      s= 7: Pi(21,10), q=30, mu = n-r = 11, parity violated
      s=11: Pi(33,16), q=48, mu = n-r = 17, parity violated
  PASS  the family Pi(3s, (3s-1)/2) with s = 3 (mod 4) has no completion for every such s, and its minimum support n-r = (3s+1)/2 is unbounded: the minimum support size does NOT govern the phenomenon

12. the amalgamation condition (PART)
-------------------------------------
  PASS  (PART) with the three groups already refutes the Pi(9,4) instance
  PASS  16 (instance, 3-partition) pairs at n = 13: (PART) is satisfied by every completable instance, as it must be
  PASS  157 3-partitions at n = 13: the three targets always agree mod 2 and at least one colour has a free parity class, so the mod-2 layer of (PART) never fires (Theorem BP3)

scope
-----
Proved here: (BC) is necessary; (BC) contains both Lemma-1 cuts and
vertex Hall; (BC) can fire exactly for k in {8,10,12,14} and never
at k = 16; vertex Hall never fires for n >= 11; (BC) is NOT
sufficient at n = 7 or 9, and the fractional relaxation is not
sufficient at n = 7.  NOT proved here: that every class-B or
class-B' instance completes at n = 13, that a simultaneous fan or an
LS(3,4,20) exists, or anything about Erdos-Rosenfeld Problem #835,
which remains open.
